e/src/index.ts:131-135`\n\n`getSummary` skips entries whose `startTime` or `endTime` fail to parse (`continue`). A legacy file storing epoch-milliseconds-as-strings (`\"1577836800000\"`) parses as `NaN` and is dropped entirely from the summary with no diagnostic — verified, that entry vanished while an adjacent non-ISO-but-parseable `\"2020-01-01 00:00:00\"` was accepted at 3600000ms. Silent omission from a *totals* report is worse than an error, because the number still looks plausible. Consider surfacing skipped entries or validating timestamps in `isEntry`.\n\n---\n\n## LOW\n\n**6. `Math.max(0, ...)` masks corrupt data rather than reporting it**\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:137`\n\nClamping negatives is reasonable defensively, but an entry with `endTime < startTime` (clock skew, hand-edited file) contributes `0` and is indistinguishable from a genuine zero-duration task. Verified: an entry spanning 2020-01-02 → 2020-01-01 yielded `{\"neg\":0}`. A comment explaining the clamp, or a distinct signal, would help.\n\n**7. Runtime type checks are dead code under the declared type signatures**\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:48` and `/workspace/src/index.ts:61`\n\n`typeof filePath !== 'string'` and `typeof taskName !== 'string'` can never be true for TypeScript callers, since both parameters are typed `string`. They are defensible as JS-consumer guards, but note the guarding is inconsistent: `getLog` does *not* apply the same treatment to `options.since`, so a JS caller passing a number or string throws a raw `TypeError: since.getTime is not a function` (verified) instead of a clear domain error. Either guard all public inputs or none.\n\n**8. `start()` trims for validation but stores the untrimmed name**\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:61-72`\n\n`'  coding  '` is accepted (correctly — it is not blank) but persisted with its surrounding whitespace, so it forms a distinct `getSummary` bucket from `'coding'`. The spec only says \"throws if empty\" (TASK.md:44), so rejecting whitespace-only is a sensible reading, but the trim-to-validate / store-raw asymmetry is likely unintentional. Decide whether the name is normalized and apply it consistently.\n\n**9. `findActive()` assumes at most one running entry; a file with several leaves orphans**\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:144-149`\n\nThe reverse scan returns only the last `endTime === null` record. If a file contains two running entries (concurrent-write artifact, per #4, or hand editing), `start()` closes only the newest and the older one stays permanently open. Verified: given running `r1` and `r2`, after `start('r3')` the active set is `['r1','r3']` — two simultaneously running tasks, which the class invariant forbids. `getSummary` will then bill `r1` against `now` forever. Consider closing *all* open entries, or repairing on load.\n\n**10. Entries are ordered by insertion, not by `startTime`**\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:107`\n\n`getLog` documents \"in the order they were started\" (line 100), but actually returns push order. These diverge for loaded data with out-of-order timestamps — verified, an entry starting in 2030 sorts ahead of one starting now. Minor, but the JSDoc overstates the guarantee; either sort or reword to \"in the order they were recorded.\"\n\n**11. Mutation safety is solid — one note**\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:29-35`, `:75`, `:90`, `:96`, `:107`\n\nI verified this holds: mutating a returned entry from `start()`, `getActiveTask()`, or `getLog()`, and even pushing into the returned array, leaves internal state untouched. Good. The only residual gap is that `cloneEntry` is a shallow copy — safe today because `Entry` is all primitives, but it will silently stop protecting anything if a nested field (e.g. `tags: string[]`) is ever added. A brief comment on `cloneEntry` noting the shallow-copy assumption would prevent that regression.\n\n**12. Unrelated `package-lock.json` churn in the diff**\n`/workspace/package-lock.json`\n\n`git diff 0449bc5 HEAD` shows 6 removed lines that are all `\"peer\": true` markers on `@types/node`, `@typescript-eslint/parser`, `acorn`, and `eslint`. This is incidental npm-resolution drift, not an intentional change, and the spec forbids adding dependencies (TASK.md:82 — not violated here, since no packages were added). Worth reverting to keep the diff limited to `src/`.\n\n---\n\n## Recommendation\n\nFinding #1 is the only one I'd call a must-fix before merge: it silently breaks the declared `Record<string, number>` return type on ordinary user input and is a two-line change. Findings #2 and #4 are the ones worth a design decision rather than a patch — they are both consequences of \"load once into memory, rewrite wholesale,\" and are the right things to discuss if this tool is expected to be invoked as a CLI multiple times against one file.\n\nRelevant files:\n- `/workspace/src/index.ts`\n- `/workspace/TASK.md`\n- `/workspace/tests/tracker.test.ts`\n- `/workspace/package-lock.json`"}],"totalDurationMs":121452,"totalTokens":28472,"totalToolUseCount":11,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":335,"cache_read_input_tokens":24295,"output_tokens":3840,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":335},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3840,"cache_read_input_tokens":24295,"cache_creation_input_tokens":335,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":335,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":7,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
